Security status
===============
This port is intentionally BROKEN. Do not remove that guard merely to test
installation on production files.

Upstream 1.0.9 (also the inspected upstream HEAD) checks file ownership and
type using pathnames, then later opens those pathnames through doas cat/dd.
It follows symlinks, does not retain a checked file descriptor, and does not
validate every parent directory. Another process can change the file or a
parent between the check and the privileged operation.

The temporary directory is created by mktemp and is private, which protects
it against other users. However, the editor and other processes of the
invoking user can replace files there. Privileged dd subsequently opens its
input by pathname. The names used for editable files, comparison copies and
the doas error log can also collide across arguments. Originals are not
checked for concurrent modifications and are truncated before copying has
successfully completed. Signal/error cleanup removes the recovery copy.

This is not a sudoedit-equivalent privilege boundary. Broad permission to
run doas dd already grants broad write access; the shell checks cannot turn
that permission into a safe restricted-file policy. No doas.conf rules are
installed or recommended by this port.

A fix needs a reviewed privileged helper that holds checked descriptors,
rejects unsafe path traversal and links, validates the unprivileged input,
detects concurrent changes, and preserves recovery data on failure. Adding
extra shell tests alone would leave the check/open races in place.
